5 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124590 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186244021 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124590 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186244022 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124590 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186244023 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124590 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186244025 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124590 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329724 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124590 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124590 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360461 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124590 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360462 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124590 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124590 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124590 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186378348 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124590 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186380284 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124590 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186380291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124590 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186575552 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124590 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Subspaces of Matrix Vector Spaces" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124590 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124401 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124590 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "8f306cf2-6881-5948-90bd-4e0ce98b4116" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124591 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049064 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124591 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186051046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124591 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125152 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124591 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124591 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186244019 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124591 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124591 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360459 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124591 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186360460 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124591 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186446400 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124591 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186481385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124591 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186496300 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124591 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124591 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551281 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124591 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551288 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124591 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Subspaces of Polynomial Vector Spaces" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124591 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124402 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124591 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "9b1bb974-826a-590f-8d32-cdc868ca827c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124707 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125348 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124707 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125349 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124707 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124707 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125525 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124707 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124707 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124707 #edb/ref 1045 "Subspaces of Abstract Vector Spaces" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124707 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186125348 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124707 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050453 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124707 #edb/ref 1050 540.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124707 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "f4bd8552-cbfe-5cdf-9c63-a1215aabe6f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125155 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477635 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186125155 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477636 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186125155 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477637 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186125155 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477638 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186125155 #edb/ref 1069 "q-43772" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125155 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125155 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R,$ and its subset $D$ containing all $2\\times 2$ matrices such that the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is equal to $1.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $D$ is closed under matrix addition\n\nII. $D$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIII. $D$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n\nIV. $D$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186125155 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bff3a0b0-c74a-55ac-a94b-e3a390164777" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125155 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. If ${A}_{1}$ and ${A}_{2}$ are two matrices from $D$ such that the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is equal to $1,$ then \n\n$$\n{A}_{1}+{A}_{2}\n$$\n\n is a matrix where the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is equal to \n\n$$\n1+1=2.\n$$\n\n This matrix does not belong to $D.$ So, $D$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. If $A$ is an element of $D$ such that the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is equal to $1,$ and $k=5\\in R,$ then \n\n$$\nkA\n$$\n\n is a matrix such that the sum of the elements on the main diagonal is equal to \n\n$$\n5\\cdot 1=5.\n$$\n\n This matrix does not belong to $D.$ So, $D$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $D$ is a *not* subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125157 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477639 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186125157 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477640 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186125157 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477641 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186125157 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477642 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186125157 #edb/ref 1069 "q-43774" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125157 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125157 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector space $C[0,1]$ of all continuous functions $f:[0,1]\\to R$ and its subset $D=\\{f\\in C[0,1]\\,|\\,f(0)=3\\}.$ \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $D$ is closed under function addition\n\nII. $D$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIII. $D$ is a subspace of $C[0,1]$\n\nIV. $D$ is **not** a subspace of $C[0,1]$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186125157 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1b6af7e1-2428-58bf-a924-9c6a5027415f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125157 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $f(x),g(x)\\in D,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)(0) & =f(0)+g(0) \\\\  & =3+3 \\\\  & =6 \\\\  & \\ne 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $D$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $f(x)\\in D$ and $k=5\\in R,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(kf)(0) & =(5f)(0) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =15 \\\\  & \\ne 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $D$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $D$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125348 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125348 #edb/ref 1061 653353 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125348 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c9024def-0495-5d67-911a-9b23824e8187" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125348 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125349 #edb/ref 1061 653361 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125349 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e3832943-8321-5094-b97b-66a6951219cb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125349 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124488 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125524 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125349 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125524 #edb/ref 1061 653358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125524 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "78c3b58c-ad85-58e8-8baf-c63bf9e544b1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125524 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124590 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125525 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125525 #edb/ref 1061 653355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125525 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f647c92d-413b-597e-a1c7-89a867776426" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125525 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125595 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125525 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125595 #edb/ref 1061 653354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125595 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fa129907-91b4-5bfa-8c65-22954ea25d25" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125595 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125858 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125762 #edb/ref 1064 "Given a vector space $V$, any subset $H$ of $V,$ which is itself a vector space, is called a **subspace** of $V$. \n\nFortunately, we do not need to check all vector space axioms to determine whether a subset of some vector space is a subspace. Instead, we only need to check the **subspace criterion**, which states the following:\n\n*A subset $H$ of a vector space $V$ is a subspace of $V$ if and only if $H$ is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication. That is to say, both of the following conditions hold:*\n\n- *If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ lie in $H$, then their sum $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}$ also lies in $H$. Formally,*\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u},\\mathbf{v} \\in H \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad  \\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v} \\in H.\n$$\n\n- *If $\\mathbf{u}$ lies in $H$, then $k \\cdot \\mathbf{u}$ also lies in $H$ for any scalar  $k$. Formally,* \n\n$$\nk \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\,\\mathbf{u} \\in H \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad  k\\cdot\\mathbf{u} \\in H.\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the vector space $\\mathbb{R}_3[t]$ containing all polynomials of degree at most $3,$ along with the subset \n\n$$\nA = \\{ at^2 \\:|\\: a \\in \\mathbb{R} \\}.\n$$\n\nIs $A$ a subspace? Let's check the two subspace criterion conditions: \n\n- Suppose $p_1(t)=a_1 t^2$ and $p_2(t)=a_2 t^2$ are two polynomials from $A$. Summing the two polynomials, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}_{1}(t)+{p}_{2}(t) & =({a}_{1}{t}^{2})+({a}_{2}{t}^{2}) \\\\  & ={{({a}_{1}+{a}_{2})}_{⏟}}_{a\\in R}{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThus, $p_1(t)+p_2(t)$ is a polynomial of the form $at^2,$ which belongs to $A$.\n\n- If $p_1(t)=a_1 t^2 \\in A$ and $k \\in \\mathbb{R},$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{p}_{1}(t) & =k({a}_{1}{t}^{2}) \\\\  & ={{(k{a}_{1})}_{⏟}}_{a\\in R}{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThus, $kp_1(t)$ is a polynomial of the form $at^2,$ which belongs to $A$.\n\nSince both conditions hold, we conclude that $A$ is a subspace $\\mathbb{R}_3[t].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125762 #edb/ref 1065 3205 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125762 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4274a05d-d402-5dfc-a339-e5a6b5a8e5b0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125762 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125858 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's now look at an example of a subset of a vector space that is not a subspace.\n\nConsider the vector space $\\mathbb R_2[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $2,$ and its subset \n\n$$\nB = \\big\\{ at+b \\: | \\: a,b  \\in\\mathbb{Z} \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nRecall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- $B$ is closed under polynomial addition. Indeed, suppose that $f(t)=a_1t+b_1$ and $g(t)=a_2t+b_2$ are two polynomials from $B.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)+g(t) & =({a}_{1}t+{b}_{1})+({a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}) \\\\  & =({a}_{1}+{a}_{2})t+({b}_{1}+{b}_{2}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a polynomial from $B$ since $a_1+a_2$ and $b_1+b_2$ are in $\\Bbb Z.$\n\n- $B$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication. As a counterexample, consider $f(t)=t$ and $k=0.5,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kf(t) & =0.5t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a polynomial from $B,$ because $0.5 \\notin \\Bbb Z.$\n\nTherefore, $B$ is not a subspace of $\\mathbb R_2[t].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125858 #edb/ref 1065 6193 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125858 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "532d5411-c233-58a0-88e3-bd13bc8fcd1e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125858 #edb/ref 1067 "Example of a Subset That Is Not a Subspace" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329725 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186451291 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186329725 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186451292 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186329725 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186451293 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186329725 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186451294 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186329725 #edb/ref 1069 "q-43776" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329725 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329725 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R,$ and its subset $L$ containing all invertible $2\\times 2$ matrices.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $L$ is closed under matrix addition\n\n- **II.** $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\n- **III.** $L$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n\n- **IV.** $L$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186329725 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "76fb5f0d-770c-53e0-bb5c-9231d8688147" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329725 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if ${A}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and ${A}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a matrix from $L.$ So, $L$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example if $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $k=0,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kA & =0\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a matrix from $L.$ So, $L$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $L$ is a *not* subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186360466 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452844 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360466 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452845 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360466 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452846 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360466 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452847 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360466 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94633" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360466 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360466 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector space $C[0,1]$ of all continuous functions $f:[0,1]\\to R$ and its subset $Q$ containing all polynomials of degree exactly $2.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $Q$ is closed under function addition\n\n- **II.** $Q$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\n- **III.** $Q$ is a subspace of $C[0,1]$\n\n- **IV.** $Q$ is **not** a subspace of $C[0,1]$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360466 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9ee30622-b701-5da4-9a44-4e5ce669fd87" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360466 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $f(x)={x}^{2}$ and $g(x)=-{x}^{2},$ then\n\n$$\nf(x)+g(x)={x}^{2}+(-{x}^{2})=0.\n$$\n\nSo, $Q$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $f(x)={x}^{2}$ and $k=0,$ then\n\n$$\nkf(x)=0\\cdot {x}^{2}=0.\n$$\n\n \nSo, $Q$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $Q$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186408166 #edb/ref 10 :answer-field.type/checkbox #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186447854 #edb/ref 1080 "false" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186447854 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186447854 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "af81d6e6-3cad-5c8a-99a9-13bc107158af" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186447855 #edb/ref 1080 "true" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186447855 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186447855 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4921fc50-ffd5-5037-85e5-4437f4da71a5" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186447856 #edb/ref 1080 "false" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186447856 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186447856 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7871ce40-048e-5347-b7c4-782324459d1f" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186447857 #edb/ref 1080 "true" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186447857 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [175921864478